Z||000000||ur2biteme||03-05-2001||05:53 AM||||<BR>i actually think the stock brakes are pretty good for spirited driving, but just need a little tweaking.  i finally got fed up with it today.  driving in the rain, i applied moderate pressure to the brakes (not stomping them, just a little harder than a gentle stop) and turned gradually to follow the curve of the road to a stop sign, and the normal wheel lockup and push i'm oh so used to in the rain happened.  the thing i've noticed time and time again is the fact that it seems like there is almost no braking from the rear.  <P>i know the front does the majority of the work, but i'm sure the rears are supposed to share the load to keep situations just like this from happening.  the brake system on my car is in good condition.  no leaks, the rotors arent excessively thin, very little if any pulsation, etc.  however, the pads are a little on the old side from lots of city driving, but i've had this wet weather problem with both old and new pads.  <P>i'm wondering how far off base i am with this idea and would like some good advice as to the topic.  i dont want to do a complete top to bottom upgrade, and there's no way i'm gonna do the grand am "upgrade" either. is there an aftermarket unit available? i dont want to start screwing with it and make it worse than it is.  thanks for the help<P>------------------<BR>Bob's '87 econo coupe<BR>aproaching 171k :)<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme" TARGET=_blank>a little piece of my madness</A>||||reg||1
